   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">Received from <name type="person" key="poun85">Ezra Pound</name>
                        </seg> the sum of £ 50 for the American serial rights of my novel <hi rend="underline">
                            <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name>
                        </hi>
                    </p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <name type="geo" key="G_CHE_ZURI">Zurich</name>: <name type="geo" key="G_CHE">Switzerland</name>
                        </dateline>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1918-12-05">5 December 1918</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">For a full discussion of this receipt and its implications, see <rs type="letter" key="L_68504">JJ to Pound, 5 December 1918</rs>, notes. Pound made two notations on the back of this receipt: “Joyce 1st receipt not accepted” and “Not accepted.” It is not exactly clear what he meant. He had been making partial installment payments to JJ on the promised £50 for the serialization of <name type="work" key="W_ULYSSES">Ulysses</name> in <name type="work" key="W_THELITTLE">The Little Review</name>, and had not at this point paid the full £50. Thus, Pound’s notations might mean that this receipt for £50, since it was given by JJ in anticipation of future full payment and so was in that respect fictitious as of the stated date, was not an acceptable financial record.</note>
            </div>
      </body>
